\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, Sing dlr at 2-week high as US dlr slides.  \b0\par\par\uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Dezember 2004\par 04:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, Dec 24 (Reuters) - The Korean won, Taiwan dollar\par and Singapore dollar rose to 2-week highs in holiday-thinned\par trading on Friday after the U.S. dollar hit fresh lows against\par the euro and ceded ground to the Japanese yen.\par     The won                   was up about 0.4 percent to near\par 1,045 per dollar, outpacing even the yen's gains since Thursday\par and putting it on track to become Asia's top performer in 2004.\par     It has risen about 14 percent so far this year. The Taiwan\par dollar, the next best performer, has managed less than half\par that gain.\par     A mixed batch of U.S. data, including weak new home sales,\par and technical factors drove the euro up on Thursday to above\par $1.35. The yen moved more cautiously, and was quoted around\par 103.68/73 at 0300 GMT.\par     Trading volumes have shrunk in the final weeks of the year.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the euro had a good chance of appreciating because\par markets had unwound their heavy euro long positions in the\par first half of December, as data from the U.S. Commodity Futures\par Commission showed.\par     "We expect the U.S. dollar to weaken further in the first\par half of 2005 against developed and emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par given the U.S. twin deficits and the perception the U.S.\par authorities are happy to see the U.S. dollar weaken, so long as\par the process is orderly," Standard Chartered Bank said in a\par commentary.\par     The Singapore dollar        moved between 1.6360 and 1.64,\par with the fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surfacing intermittently.\par     Even offshore premiums on the Chinese yuan were higher as\par markets disregarded a statement from authorities earlier this\par week that they had no plans to shift their stance on the\par tightly managed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Speculation has intensified in the market that China might\par shift its stance before the end of the year. But a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par spokesman said on Wednesday, "At present there is no change in\par the exchange rate policy."\par     One-year non-deliverable forwards           were quoted at\par 4,900 points, pricing in a 6.3 percent appreciation in the yuan\par in a year. The yuan is effectively pegged around 8.28 per U.S.\par dollar.\par     "There is no extra special news that has triggered the\par speculation. I think it is the broad dollar trend," said Tai\par Hui, Standard Chartered Bank's economist for Greater China,\par referring to the move in NDFs.\par  \par     Standard Chartered expects China to widen the yuan's narrow\par trading band in the first half of 2005.\par     Meanwhile, with the Taiwan dollar around 32.05 and close to\par the key 32-per-U.S.-dollar level, markets were treading warily\par fearing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Hui said 32 was a level to watch. Yet recent economic data\par had been strong and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might not be averse to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gains, he said.\par     Taiwan's export orders showed a robust 30 percent annual\par growth in November.\par     "Export orders data released yesterday was still quite\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 strong," said Hui. "From that perspective, I don't think the  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is concerned about the level. Smoothing out the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ups and downs in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might be their objective."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets in the Philippines were closed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian rupiah traded conclusively on the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 firmer side of 9,300 for the first time in a week, seemingly  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 overcoming recent downward pressures from commercial outflows,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 political and security worries.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0321 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       103.66         103.95       +0.28\par  Sing dlr        1.6397         1.6411       +0.09\par  Taiwan dlr      32.174         32.228       +0.17\par  Korean won     1046.00        1050.40       +0.42\par  Baht             39.01          39.03       +0.05\par  Peso             56.08          56.13       +0.09\par  Rupiah         9283.00        9290.00       +0.08\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       103.66         107.31       +3.52\par  Sing dlr        1.6397         1.6978       +3.54\par  Taiwan dlr      32.174         33.978       +5.61\par  Korean won     1046.00        1192.00      +13.96\par  Baht             39.01          39.59       +1.47\par  Peso             56.08          55.49       -1.05\par  Rupiah         9283.00        8410.00       -9.40\par  Rupee            43.76          45.60       +4.21.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041