\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Down Late After Rumored MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 476 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2004\par 10:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar was slightly weaker in Asia late Thursday as rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  overcame heavy U.S. dollar-selling by various banks.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds were slightly lower in thin trading ahead of the Christmas holiday.  \par \par \uc2 At 0745 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6416, up slightly from S$1.6410 late in Asia Wednesday. It traded between S$1.6369 and S$1.6417 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the U.S. dollar plunged as low as S$1.6369, its lowest since Dec. 8, as some Singapore-based banks sold the U.S. unit on behalf of corporate customers seeking to make last-minute position adjustments ahead of the year-end holidays.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide against the local dollar immediately ran into buying interest from domestic banks, which were apparently acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar buying by local banks continued through the morning, and by early afternoon, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been pushed as high as S$1.6418.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the MAS may continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market later in the global day if the U.S. dollar resumes its fall against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This could happen if tonight's slew of U.S. economic data - including November durable goods orders - disappoints market participants.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. durable goods orders are expected to have staged a modest rebound in November. The median finding of analysts surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for a 0.6% increase in durable orders after a 1.1% decrease in October.  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore government bond market, meanwhile, was very subdued ahead of the Christmas holiday in the U.S. on Friday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 For 2005, DBS Bank said in a research report that it recommends going long on government bonds in those Asian economies with fundamental \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, such as Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 'In particular, since SGS (Singapore Government Securities) will receive an extra boost from the inclusion of SGS in Citigroup's World Government Bond Index (WGBI), launching in Jan. 2005, SGS will remain bullish,' DBS said.  \par \par \uc2 The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Latest  Previous \par Two-year bond yield  1.60%   1.58% \par 10-year bond yield   2.62%   2.59% \par Two-year swap offer  1.76%   1.72% \par 10-year swap offer   2.85%   2.80% \par 2-10-yr swap spread: 109 BPs 108 BPs \par  \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Paul Baylis  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041223e0cn0056b\par }\p