\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late After US Dlr Falls Vs Most Majors  \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2004\par 09:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger in Asia late Tuesday in the wake of the U.S. dollar's weakness against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were flat to lower in subdued trade ahead of the Christmas holiday later this week, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0805 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6420, down from S$1.6457 late in Asia Monday. It traded between S$1.6390 and S$1.6428 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell against most of its major rivals late Monday, U.S. funds sold U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars at the start of Tuesday's Asian session, pushing the U.S. unit as low as S$1.6390 in the morning.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the U.S. funds appeared to be placing fresh bearish-U.S.-dollar bets versus the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the prevailing market view for further weakness of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 But domestic banks soon stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy the U.S. unit around the S$1.6390 level, lifting it as high as S$1.6428 later in the morning.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation was rife that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  via local banks to prop up the U.S. dollar against its Singaporean counterpart. Since the middle of last week, the MAS has been rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  occasionally, either to stem the U.S. dollar's slide against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or to curb excessive volatility of the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit then consolidated steadily above the psychologically important S$1.6400 level in the afternoon, and is likely to stay above S$1.6388, which is the support on the minor uptrend line from the Dec. 15 trough, for the rest of the session.  \par \par \uc2 Based on technical analysis, the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair is forming a symmetrical triangle bounded by the minor uptrend line (now at S$1.6388) from the Dec. 15 low and by the downtrend line (now at S$1.6477) from the Dec. 10 peak. The downtrend in 10-day momentum suggests the most likely direction of any breakout would be down, which would immediately target the Dec. 15 low of S$1.6370.  \par \par \uc2 The following are yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Latest  Previous \par Two-year bond yield  1.53%   1.53% \par 10-year bond yield   2.55%   2.53% \par Two-year swap offer  1.72%   1.72% \par 10-year swap offer   2.80%   2.86% \par 2-10-yr swap spread: 108 BPs 114 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lena Lee [ 21-12-04 0827GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041221e0cl000an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
