\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar up on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  hints, Asian shares dip.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Callus  \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2004\par 09:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Dec 7 (Reuters) - The dollar rallied on Tuesday on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hints, underpinning shares in Asian exporters, but stocks in the region were mostly lower on nerves over Japan economic data and Hong Kong IPOs due this week.  \par \par \uc2 Shares in Amcor, the world's largest maker of plastic bottles, fell 8 percent on news it had ousted its chief executive after finding some staff may have acted illegally to drive up box prices.  \par \par \uc2 Crude oil continued a rally from three-month lows to stand above $43 a barrel, as traders eyed signals from OPEC that it might take action to stem a slide that knocked 14 percent off prices last week. The producer cartel meets in Cairo on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Gold weakened as the dollar climbed, and Japanese government bonds were flat ahead of economic data out Wednesday and Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euro-zone finance ministers built on recent rhetoric against their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surge, saying in a joint statement that sharp moves in exchange rates were unwelcome and that they were closely monitoring swings.  \par \par \uc2 "It hints at \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they (the ECB and European finance ministries) together control \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy for Europe," said Jake Moore, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's getting a bit nervous and that's prompted a bit of profit-taking," he said, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would probably not occur unless the euro rose to around $1.38.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was around $1.3408 against the euro up slightly from late levels in New York, where it rose about 0.4 percent. It marked a record low of $1.3461 on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought around 103.02 yen little changed from late New York, where it rose 1 yen, due in part to concerns about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It was well off a five-year low of 101.83 yen hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 GDP, IPOS AWAITED  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei stock average fell 0.25 percent to 10,954.01 by its mid-session close, Hong Kong's Hang Seng was up 0.14 percent at 14,283.58 in early trade, while an MSCI index of Asian non-Japan shares was 0.46 percent lower at 255.79.  \par \par \uc2 Mitsui Trust Holdings Inc, Japan's seventh-largest bank by assets, supported the Nikkei, climbing 4.1 percent after Goldman Sachs raised its rating on the stock to "outperform" from "in line". The broker said continued operating profit growth would give it enough retained earnings to pay back the funds it owes from a state bail-out in the late 1990s. Daiei Inc. jumped 7 percent, extending gains into a fifth straight session as foreign and domestic investors, including Wal-Mart Stores Inc., queued up to submit proposals to the state-backed Industrial Revitalisation Corp. of Japan (IRCJ) for reviving the debt-laden retailer.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo traders said investors were on the sidelines ahead of revised gross domestic product (GDP) figures for the July-September quarter, due on Wednesday, and October machinery orders data due on Thursday to get a better idea of how Japan's economy is faring.  \par \par \uc2 "The Nikkei is basically stuck around 11,000 and we're all waiting for a signal for which way to go next," said Koji Muneoka, head of domestic sales trading at HSBC in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 BIG HK IPOS  \par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong all eyes were on two large IPOs - Air China and the Hong Kong Housing Authority's Link's property trust - due to raise a combined US$4.1 billion this week. Singapore's Straits Times rose half a percent, led by Sembcorp, which announced a special dividend. Taiwan's benchmark index dropped a third of a percent ahead of legislative elections on Saturday. Australia's S&P ASX 200 fell 0.31, dragged down by Amcor. South Korea's KOSPI fell 0.61 percent. Global stocks as measured by the MSCI World Index were down 0.36 percent.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks offering no clear direction to Asian markets, closing mixed. The Dow Jones industrial average ended down 0.43 percent at 10,547.06 as oil prices nudged higher. The Nasdaq Composite Index finished up 0.15 percent at 2,151.25 as Apple Computer Inc. rose 5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Gold fell to $452.75 an ounce from $454 overnight. January NYMEX crude climbed 8 cents to $43.06. Ten-year JGB futures were flat, yielding 1.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041207e0c70069b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
