\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up At Another 6-Yr High Vs US Dlr Late  \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 09:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose to another six-year high against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Friday, boosted by speculator-led buying spurred by a newspaper report that China had cut its U.S. bond holdings.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were higher late Friday, the result of continued bull-flattening trades in the local market, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6390, down from S$1.6404 in Asia late Thursday. It traded between S$1.6376 and S$1.6409 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After falling to S$1.6382 on selling by U.S. investment banks in the morning, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged down further to S$1.6376 in the afternoon following a Chinese newspaper report quoting the People's Bank of China's policy board member Yu Yongding as saying China has reduced its U.S. bond holdings to about US$180 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The newspaper report didn't provide details of China's previous holdings of U.S. bonds, though the report said around 80% of China's estimated US$515 billion worth of foreign reserves are usually held in U.S. dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the newspaper report also generated worries that China could further decrease its holdings of U.S. Treasurys, which would likely lead to more U.S. dollar weakness against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the local dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to garner support from rumored buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore in the high-S$1.6300s throughout the session. Recently, the MAS has apparently stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars, due to enhanced bearishness toward the U.S. unit, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 As a result, speculators who had sold the U.S. dollar down to S$1.6376 in the afternoon bought back the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the session to take profit on their short positions, lifting the U.S. unit up to near the psychologically important S$1.6400 level. [ 26-11-04 0840GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Prices of Singapore government bonds were mostly higher late Friday on continued buying mainly at the long-end, resulting in a further flattening of the yield curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the local session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was flat at 1.51%. At the longer end of the curve, the 10-year yield fell 3 basis points to 2.78%, while the 15-year yield fell 5 basis points to 3.17%.  \par \par \uc2 In the interest rate swaps market, the two-year swap offer was unchanged at 1.67% from late Thursday, the five-year swap offer was at 2.32% versus 2.33%, while the 10-year offer fell to 2.99% from 3.01%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 132 basis points from 134 basis points late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 In the interbank market, the overnight rate inched up to 1.1250% from 0.6250% late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change.  \par \par \uc2 Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 26-11-04 0850GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041126e0bq000d6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
