\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up At Another 6-Yr High Vs US Dlr Late  \b0\par\par\uc2 353 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2004\par 09:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose to another six-year high against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Friday, boosted by speculator-led buying spurred by a newspaper report that China had cut its U.S. bond holdings.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were higher late Friday, the result of continued bull-flattening trades in the local market, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6390, down from S$1.6404 in Asia late Thursday. It traded between S$1.6376 and S$1.6409 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After falling to S$1.6382 on selling by U.S. investment banks in the morning, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged down further to S$1.6376 in the afternoon following a Chinese newspaper report quoting the People's Bank of China's policy board member Yu Yongding as saying China has reduced its U.S. bond holdings to about US$180 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The newspaper report didn't provide details of China's previous holdings of U.S. bonds, though the report said around 80% of China's estimated US$515 billion worth of foreign reserves are usually held in U.S. dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the newspaper report also generated worries that China could further decrease its holdings of U.S. Treasurys, which would likely lead to more U.S. dollar weakness against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the local dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to garner support from rumored buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore in the high-S$1.6300s throughout the session. Recently, the MAS has apparently stepped up its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars, due to enhanced bearishness toward the U.S. unit, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 As a result, speculators who had sold the U.S. dollar down to S$1.6376 in the afternoon bought back the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the session to take profit on their short positions, lifting the U.S. unit up to near the psychologically important S$1.6400 level.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041126e0bq0053f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
