\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late On Purchase By US Funds; Bonds Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 08:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger in Asia late Wednesday as U.S. funds used bullish remarks on the baht by Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra as a reason to buy the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were also higher, mainly due to lower interbank rates and dip-buying interest, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0640 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6431, down from S$1.6465 in Asia late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6420 and S$1.6448 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Given rife speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Tuesday to stem the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide to a fresh six-year low of S$1.6414, the U.S. unit kicked off Wednesday's Asian session on a positive note near the mid-S$1.6400 level.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. dollar dropped as low as S$1.6420 in the morning after Thaksin said that the strong baht shouldn't have much adverse effect on Thailand's exports. Thai exporters must adapt to a stronger baht as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to rise further against the U.S. dollar, he said.  \par \par \uc2 'U.S. funds sold dollar-Sing and dollar-baht' on Thaksin's comments, said a Singapore dealer.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said Thaksin's comments on the baht may have sparked similar notions among U.S. funds that Singapore authorities may be resigned to a stronger local dollar and won't do much to support the beleaguered U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Early in the afternoon, the U.S. dollar recovered slightly to around S$1.6435 levels on its mild rebound against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the yen.  \par \par \uc2 For the rest of the week, the U.S. unit is likely to trade in a relatively narrow S$1.6400-S$1.6480 range, United Overseas Bank said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 'With the euro struggling above US$1.3100, the U.S. dollar still above Y103.00 and talk of MAS in the market, the dollar-Sing's downside should be limited to S$1.6400 for now,' it said.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041124e0bo004bn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
