\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late After Snow's Remarks On  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2004\par 09:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger in Asia late Thursday, bolstered by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's remarks that suggest the U.S. won't take steps to support the ailing U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were mostly higher, especially at the long end, in line with overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0715 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6453, down from S$1.6502 in Asia late Wednesday. It traded between S$1.6450 and S$1.6470 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, Snow said 'the history of efforts to impose non-market valuations on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at best been unrewarding and checkered.'  \par \par \uc2 He added that 'no one has ever devalued their way to prosperity' and the 'only way to get prosperity is to follow the marketplace,' although he did reiterate his support for a 'strong dollar policy.'  \par \par \uc2 Overall, Snow's comments reinforced a market perception that the U.S. government is tolerant of the U.S. dollar's weakness, prompting some non-Singapore investment banks to drive the U.S. unit down to S$1.6450 early in the session.  \par \par \uc2 This was followed by rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a further fall of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sparked light profit-taking on short U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar positions and lifted the U.S. unit back up to around S$1.6465.  \par \par \uc2 But despite talk of MAS supporting the U.S. dollar over the past few days, the risk is that it will trade lower in the coming sessions, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 UOB cited the U.S. dollar's broad-based weakness and expectations that the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations could at this weekend's meeting in Berlin put more pressure on China to allow the yuan to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 'For now, we expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair to hold at S$1.6400, with the upside likely to be capped at around S$1.6520-S$1.6530,' UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 Technical analysis points to a further U.S. dollar fall against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term after negative 10-day momentum divergence Wednesday, which implies the U.S. unit may retest initial support at S$1.6448 as soon as later in the global day.  \par \par \uc2 Any clear break of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below S$1.6448 would immediately target secondary support in the S$1.6420-S$1.6430 area, which consists of the lows set in early December 1998.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041118e0bi004h7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
