\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late On Bullishness; Outlook Strong  \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2004\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Wednesday, underpinned by bullish sentiment toward the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it hit a fresh near six-year high in overseas markets late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were steady in extremely thin trade, as players ignored the overnight slide in U.S. Treasurys and weaker-than-expected Singapore gross domestic product and export data.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6502, down from S$1.6526 in Asia late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6490 and S$1.6518 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Throughout the session, the U.S. dollar was caught in a tug-of-war between Singapore dollar bulls seeking to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any upticks and participants wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumored to have stepped into the market late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the MAS apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars when the U.S. unit slid as low as S$1.6475 late Tuesday, its lowest since December 1998.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, analysts said the U.S. dollar is likely to fall further against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite signs that Singapore's economy is slowing.  \par \par \uc2 'For now, the risk remains on the downside for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair, with the market targeting S$1.6450,' said United Overseas Bank in a research report, citing ongoing concerns about the huge U.S. fiscal and current account deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, ING Financial Markets said it expects the U.S. dollar to decline to S$1.6370 in the next three months.  \par \par \uc2 Early Wednesday, the Ministry of Trade & Industry said Singapore's GDP grew by 7.5% in the third quarter from a year ago, narrower than the government's earlier estimate of 7.7% expansion. The government also narrowed its full-year growth forecast to 8.0%-8.5% from an earlier 8%-9%.  \par \par \uc2 International Enterprise Singapore also said Wednesday that the city-state's non-oil domestic exports rose 11.9% on year to S$11.96 billion in October, which was below the average forecast of a 14.2% gain by eight economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041117e0bh004v1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
