\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Weaker with yen, waiting on Korea.  \b0\par\par\uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2004\par 02:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  softened on\par Thursday after a sharp fall in the yen and some gains in the\par dollar on a narrowing of the U.S. trade deficit and the Federal\par Reserve's fourth rate rise this year.\par     The Korean won                  pulled back from a\par seven-year high hit this week, helped by some suspected\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar was about one cent off a\par near-five-year high seen on Monday.\par     The Taiwan dollar          extended its retreat from\par Tuesday's seven-month high of 32.85 per dollar, while the\par Philippine peso  and Thai baht        were hampered by\par domestic worries.\par     The yen        retreated 1 percent against the dollar in\par offshore trade, to back over 107 per dollar, and that helped\par pull most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lower.\par     "There was lots of suspicion that Japan's monetary\par authorities were involved in the move, but no evidence to\par support that speculation," BNP Paribas analysts said in a\par report.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Asian trade was thinned by a holiday in Singapore, one the\par region's major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading centres. Monday is also\par a holiday in Singapore, so trading was expected to be subdued\par until Tuesday with many in the market taking an extended\par weekend.\par     KOREAN RATES\par     While the Fed raised rates and suggested there were more\par rate rises to come, markets in South Korea were waiting to see\par if there would be a rate cut by the Bank of Korea. A Reuters\par poll found economist were not expecting a cut this month.\par     The Bank surprised markets with a cut in August due to\par sluggish domestic activity, but has since held the overnight\par call rate at 3.5 percent due to concerns about rising\par inflation.\par     "While ultimately we accept the need for the Bank of Korea\par to further lower rates it is a little premature to think it\par will do that at this point," UBS strategist Ashley Davies said.\par     "A rate cut would send the wrong signal on inflation\par expectations given that core inflation has risen to a 19-month\par high on the back of higher oil prices."\par     After the won rallied to 1,102 per dollar on Tuesday, its\par strongest in nearly seven years, Korean authorities were\par suspected to have bought the dollar to cap the gains.\par     On Thursday, ahead of the decision, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official\par said authorities were concerned about one-sided movements in\par dollar/won.\par     Davies said despite despite economists expecting no change\par in rates, some market participants were, so steady policy could\par see the won soften a little.\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par  \par  Change on the day at   0143 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.96         106.02       -0.88\par  Sing dlr        1.6588         1.6556       -0.19\par  Taiwan dlr      33.180         33.117       -0.19\par  Korean won     1112.00        1110.30       -0.15\par  Baht             40.74          40.73       -0.02\par  Peso             56.32          56.34       +0.04\par  Rupiah         9080.00        9036.00       -0.48\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highl