\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late As US Dlr Bearishness Persists  \b0\par\par\uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 09:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose slightly against its U.S. counterpart late Tuesday in Asia, bolstered by lingering bearish sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Local government bond prices were slightly higher, as bond yields fell in line with falls in local interest-rate swaps amid strong receiving interest across the swap curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0805 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6525, down from S$1.6535 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.6497 and S$1.6539 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After falling to a five-year low of S$1.6493 late Monday in New York, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded to S$1.6539 early in the Asian session on the unwinding of short U.S. dollar positions by some domestic and foreign banks to take profits.  \par \par \uc2 But by late morning, the U.S. unit had slumped back to the low-S$1.6500s, as other players sold into its rebound amid overall U.S. dollar bearishness, before consolidating around S$1.6520 for the rest of the session.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said worries over the massive U.S. "twin" deficits -budget and trade - and speculation that China may move to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the near term, continued to weigh on the U.S. dollar while buoying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has risen roughly 1.4% against the U.S. unit since rumors of an imminent revaluation of the Chinese yuan intensified last week, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 Further U.S. dollar declines are expected, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market expecting the yuan to appreciate 3%-5% in the next three to six months, UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 Technical charts also appear to support this, with the general bearish outlook likely to result in further lows for the U.S. dollar with respect to the Singapore dollar, UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers warned that a further downside could be limited should the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore step in to restrict the slide, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts pointed to an increase in the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in September as evidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may already have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore's official foreign reserves were up about 3%, to US$105.83 billion at the end of October, from US$102.75 billion in September, according to preliminary data issued by the MAS Monday. [ 09-11-04 0828GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds ended marginally higher late Tuesday, in tandem with a broad rise in U.S. Treasurys overnight and aided by strong receiving interest in local interest rate swaps, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But trade was generally modest, especially ahead of Wednesday's U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meeting.  \par \par \uc2 With the Federal Reserve expected to raise key interest rates by 25 basis points - and possibly another 25 basis points at the next meeting in December - government bond yields could head higher, UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 "As most of the economic data is out of the way, SGS is likely to track external developments for now," with the two-year yield moving toward 1.60%, while the longer-dated 10-year yield could rise to 3.3%, UOB added.  \par \par \uc2 Late in the session, the yield on two-year Singapore government bonds was down 1 basis point at 1.55%, while the 10-year bond yield was also 1 basis point lower at 3.15%. The 15-year bond yield fell 3 basis points to 3.58% by the end of the session.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were also mostly lower, after seeing some heavy receiving interest right across the swap curve ahead of the FOMC meeting, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The two-year swap offer was at 1.70%, versus 1.74% late Monday. The five-year swap offer fell 5 basis points to 2.51% from 2.56%, while the 10-year offer fell to 3.35% from 3.39%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps was unchanged at 165 basis points from late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 In the interbank market, the overnight rate was up at 1.2500% from late Monday's 1.1250%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and subject to change.  \par \par \uc2 Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 09-11-04 0842GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041109e0b9000lj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
