\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late As US Dlr Bearishness Persists  \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose slightly against its U.S. counterpart late Tuesday in Asia, bolstered by lingering bearish sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Local government bond prices were slightly higher, as bond yields fell in line with falls in local interest-rate swaps amid strong receiving interest across the swap curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0805 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6525, down from S$1.6535 late Monday in Asia. It traded between S$1.6497 and S$1.6539 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After falling to a five-year low of S$1.6493 late Monday in New York, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded to S$1.6539 early in the Asian session on the unwinding of short U.S. dollar positions by some domestic and foreign banks to take profits.  \par \par \uc2 But by late morning, the U.S. unit had slumped back to the low-S$1.6500s, as other players sold into its rebound amid overall U.S. dollar bearishness, before consolidating around S$1.6520 for the rest of the session.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said worries over the massive U.S. 'twin' deficits -budget and trade - and speculation that China may move to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime in the near term, continued to weigh on the U.S. dollar while buoying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has risen roughly 1.4% against the U.S. unit since rumors of an imminent revaluation of the Chinese yuan intensified last week, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 Further U.S. dollar declines are expected, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market expecting the yuan to appreciate 3%-5% in the next three to six months, UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 Technical charts also appear to support this, with the general bearish outlook likely to result in further lows for the U.S. dollar with respect to the Singapore dollar, UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers warned that a further downside could be limited should the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore step in to restrict the slide, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts pointed to an increase in the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in September as evidence that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may already have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars for \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore's official foreign reserves were up about 3%, to US$105.83 billion at the end of October, from US$102.75 billion in September, according to preliminary data issued by the MAS Monday.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041109e0b900692\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
