\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Steady after Oct surge, wait on US.  \b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 November 2004\par 10:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Rajat Bhattacharya\par     SINGAPORE, Nov 1 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  steadied at\par strong levels on Monday, after surging against the dollar\par through October, as investors refrained from placing new bets\par ahead of a closely contested U.S. presidential election.\par     The South Korean won            strengthened to a new\par four-year high against the dollar in local trade before losing\par some ground, after a 3 percent gain in October. The Singapore\par dollar  traded near a six-month high while the Taiwan\par dollar approached it's highest level in almost five months.\par     "Everybody's looking at the U.S. presidential elections,"\par said Jimmy Koh, head of treasury research at Singapore's United\par Overseas Bank.\par     "While the consensus is to buy the dollar if a clear winner\par emerges, the outcome of the elections remains uncertain.\par Eventually, the widening U.S. fiscal and trade deficits will\par weigh on the dollar."\par     President George W.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Bush and Democratic contender John\par Kerry are tied nationwide ahead of Tuesday's elections,\par according to a Reuters/Zogby poll released on Sunday.\par     Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have surged over the past month, led by a\par 5 percent surge in the yen  against the dollar, and a 3.4\par percent gain of the Singapore dollar , as concerns about\par record high oil prices and the yawning U.S. current account\par deficits weighed on the dollar.\par     U.S. data on Friday showed gross domestic product grew at\par an annualised rate of 3.7 percent in the third quarter, falling\par short of analysts' expectations, while an index of U.S.\par consumer sentiment was stronger than expected.\par     If U.S. non-farm payrolls, due on Friday, were weak, it\par could prompt the Federal Reserve to hold back on raising\par interest rates, hurting the dollar against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par said BNP Paribas in a report.\par     The bank expects the Singapore dollar to test its six-month\par high of about 1.66 per dollar before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . It traded on Monday at 1.6642/52 per U.S.\par dollar.\par  \par     "We reckon it's a matter of time before this level gives\par way, towards the next support at 1.65," BNP said.\par     The Singapore \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which conducts monetary policy\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by guiding the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within its undisclosed trade-weighted  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 band, has said it will allow a gradual and modest appreciation  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Worries about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept the South Korean won from  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 extending its early morning gains. Adding to market caution, a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 senior South Korean commerce ministry official said a stronger  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 won could hurt the economy's competitiveness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments came after the won hit a four-year high on  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 data showing core inflation jumped to a 19-month high in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October and exports grew a faster-than-expected 20.9 percent in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the year through October. This boosted expectations the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central  \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may hold back further rate cuts at its meeting next week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While the trend is still of monetary easing, there's a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 50-50 chance of no change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 policy," said Christy Tan, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at 4CAST Ltd.   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at   0800 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.39         105.97       -0.39\par  Sing dlr        1.6643         1.6630       -0.08\par  Taiwan dlr      33.457         33.435       -0.07\par  Korean won     1118.90        1119.00       +0.01\par  Baht             41.08          41.02       -0.15\par  Peso             56.32          56.32       +0.01\par  Rupiah         9087.00        9068.00       -0.21\par  Rupee            45.39          45.40       +0.03\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.39         107.31       +0.86\par  Sing dlr        1.6643         1.6978       +2.01\par  Taiwan dlr      33.457         33.978       +1.56\par  Korean won     1118.90        1192.00       +6.53\par  Baht             41.08          39.59       -3.64\par  Peso             56.32          55.49       -1.46\par  Rupiah         9087.00        8410.00       -7.45\par  Rupee            45.39          45.60       +0.46.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041101e0b1006kl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoa