\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Rally despite oil, stocks - won hits high.  \b0\par\par\uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2004\par 11:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Vidya Ranganathan\par     SINGAPORE, Oct 25 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rose on\par Monday, with the Korean won hitting a four-year high, as the\par region ignored high oil prices and falling stock markets to\par claim ground from a declining U.S. dollar.\par     Worries about the widening U.S. trade deficit and the\par approaching U.S. presidential election drove the dollar to an\par eight-month low against the euro  and a six-month trough\par against the yen .\par     The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was up one yen since Friday at\par 106.70/75, undeterred by the nearly 2 percent fall in the\par Nikkei average  and Saturday's massive earthquake.\par     The won                  met with some resistance near\par 1,135 a dollar, a November 2000 peak and a rise of half a\par percent since Friday, prompting speculation that the Bank of\par Korea was \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rein in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     A \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -based trader said spikes in the won on the\par higher side of 1,135 were short-lived, suggesting won-selling\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Sing dollar        hit 1.6620, stopping short of an\par April high near 1.66. A break above that would take it to its\par strongest levels since January 2000.\par  \par     Markets in Thailand were closed for a holiday and a typhoon\par forced businesses in Taiwan to close.\par     "The threat of the Korean authorities has been enough to\par keep dollar/won constrained, and the market is starting to fear\par the Bank of Japan," IDEAglobal.com said in a commentary.\par     "As such, further (dollar/won) downside is still possible,\par driven obviously by the majors but also good export orders,"\par IDEAglobal.com said.\par     Economists at UBS said the won's appreciation had been\par slower than that of most other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the majors.\par     "The BoK was rumoured to be present around the 1,135 level\par but the bid was not heard to be strong," UBS said in a note.\par     The underperformance has driven the won to a three-month\par trough of about 10.67 per yen, down from 10.28 at the end of\par September.\par     The spike in oil prices weighed on the region's stock\par markets , dragging Korean shares to an eight-week\par low and Philippine stocks to a one-month low.\par     The Philippine peso        lagged the region, rising very\par marginally to 56.26 a dollar. The Indonesian rupiah  also\par managed meagre gains.\par     SINGAPORE INFLATION\par     Singapore released data showing consumer prices rose a\par faster-than-expected 0.5 percent in September over August, as\par oil prices boosted transport and food costs.\par     "It remains a challenge for authorities in Asia to control\par the second-round effects of oil price inflation," UBS said.\par     "Therefore authorities in economies where the output gap\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 continues to close despite a modest slowdown in growth - China,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India, Singapore, Thailand and Taiwan - have all been biased  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 towards tightening monetary conditions in one way or the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 other."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS said the volatility of output in Singapore had,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 however, fallen and that meant the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore was likely to maintain the tightening bias it  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 announced in April for a long time.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The MAS runs monetary policy by guiding the Sing dollar\par within an undisclosed trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "According to UBS's calculations, the Sing dollar nominal\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 effective rate is still one percent below the top end of the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 basket in which the MAS tracks this \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the pace of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 appreciation may invite the MAS' hand," UBS said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS recommended booking profits on long Sing dollar  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 positions and waiting for better levels to reestablish that  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 bullish Sing view.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  Change on the day at   0808 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.51         107.57       +1.00\par  Sing dlr        1.6619         1.6679       +0.36\par  Taiwan dlr      33.796         33.796       +0.00\par  Korean won     1134.80        1140.70       +0.52\par  Baht             41.02          41.27       +0.61\par  Peso             56.30          56.32       +0.04\par  Rupiah         9069.00        9098.00       +0.32\par  Rupee            45.64          45.65       +0.01\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       106.51         107.31       +0.75\par  Sing dlr        1.6619         1.6978       +2.16\par  Taiwan dlr      33.796         33.978      