\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late, Hits 6-Mo High As USD Falls  \b0\par\par\uc2 341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2004\par 11:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was sharply higher late Friday amid a U.S. dollar-bearish market in Asia, breaking a key resistance level and hitting a fresh six-month high.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices were either flat or slightly lower, with some tenors tracking an overnight fall in U.S. Treasurys. Overall trade, however, was subdued ahead of the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 At 0815 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6688, down from S$1.6720 late Thursday in Asia. It traded between S$1.6680 and S$1.6743 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 At the start of the local session, the U.S. dollar was around S$1.6740, supported by talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight to keep the U.S. unit from falling too sharply against the local dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But the speculation soon faded as the market's focus returned to the U.S. current account deficit and how higher oil prices could affect U.S. economic and monetary policies.  \par \par \uc2 With the U.S. dollar resuming its downtrend against the yen and euro, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later broke below the psychologically and technically important S$1.6700 level.  \par \par \uc2 At 0815 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at Y107.58 versus Y107.56 in New York overnight, while the euro was at US$1.2630, up from US$1.2603.  \par \par \uc2 In Singapore, the U.S. dollar extended its fall to the session low of S$1.6680, its weakest level since April 13, the day after the MAS moved to a tightening policy bias by favoring a stronger Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, dealers expect the U.S. dollar to fall further in the coming sessions on concerns over rising oil prices and their impact on the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 UOB Treasury said in a research report that some players were already 'talking about S$1.6650, then S$1.6610,' and suggested that a break below Y107 could be a possible trigger.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041022e0am006hi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
