\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Asia:Singapore Dlr Hasn't Caught Up With Fundamentals  \b0\par\par\uc2 913 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2004\par 07:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Ron Harui and Kevin Lim \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar is stubbornly refusing to rise despite strong economic growth and creeping inflation in the city-state. But analysts are confident the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will gain in the coming months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Major banks expect the Singapore dollar to rise 2% to 3% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of the year as the market catches up with Singapore's fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "Our longstanding view has been that the Singapore dollar's secular trend is biased towards appreciation, because of its fundamental support - economic recovery, a strong external balance and fiscal prudence," Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi said in a recent report.  \par \par \uc2 The island's economy grew 7.5% on year in the first quarter and is expected to accelerate further, while prices are edging higher. And yet the Singapore dollar has weakened in the three months since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed policy and targeted a rise in the local unit.  \par \par \uc2 Factors weighing down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  include the U.S. dollar's resilience in the face of America's widening trade and budget deficits, and weak sentiment toward Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to concerns over a hard landing for China's white-hot economy.  \par \par \uc2 Uncertainty arising from Monday's Indonesian presidential election, which is expected to drag on to a September runoff, has also been cited as a reason for weakness in Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But United Overseas Bank treasury economist Jimmy Koh noted this didn't stop the rupiah from hitting a six-month high this week.  \par \par \uc2 Given the Singapore dollar's weakness during the past few months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumored to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on several occasions to boost the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . MAS may also have tightened liquidity in the local money markets to bolster the Singapore dollar, J.P. Morgan said in a research note Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        U.S. Dlr May Fall To S$1.6600 End-Dec \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , citing growing inflationary pressures, shifted April 12 from a policy of "zero appreciation" of the Singapore dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to seeking "modest and gradual appreciation."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The MAS targets the local dollar's exchange rate, rather than interest rates, to spur or damp the economy because trade flows dwarf the island's small domestic economy. The U.S. dollar and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pegged to it - such as the Malaysian ringgit, Hong Kong dollar and Chinese yuan - are believed to account for more than half of MAS's undisclosed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's desire for a stronger local unit and the fundamental case for such a rise, the U.S. dollar has risen 2.8% Since April 12 to S$1.7128 around 0450 GMT Wednesday. Against the MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket, UOB reckons the local dollar is 0.3% weaker than the midpoint of allowed trading band, compared with just above the pivot before MAS tightened monetary policy, and 1.5% above the center in the "immediate aftermath" of the monetary policy statement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But eventually, analysts say, the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should appreciate again - as the MAS wants.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is really little reason why the Singapore dollar (trade-weighted rate) should trade around current levels," UOB said in a report Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UOB thinks the Singapore dollar should be worth around S$1.7034 to the U.S. dollar based on a level of 0.5% above the midpoint of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  band. The bank expects the U.S. dollar to fall to S$1.6900 by the end of September and S$1.6700 by year's end.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DBS Bank projects the U.S. dollar will be at S$1.6700 at the end of September and S$1.6600 at the end of the December. The U.S. dollar's trading range should be closer to S$1.6800-S$1.7000 than its current S$1.7000-S$1.7200, given the strong local stock market, strong economic numbers coming out of Singapore and the rise of inflation to the 2% level, said DBS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist Philip Wee.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A bit less Singapore dollar-bullish is Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, which sees the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  falling to S$1.7050 by the end of September and S$1.6800 at the end of December.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other players predicting a stronger Singapore dollar include Morgan Stanley, which forecasts the U.S. dollar falling to S$1.6700 at year's end, and J.P. Morgan, which tips the U.S. unit at S$1.6600 by then.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a long period of sluggish growth following the 1997 Asian economic crisis, with two recessions thrown in for good measure, Singapore has roared back to life with double-digit growth in exports and retail spending since the start of this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After the strong first quarter, gross domestic product for the second quarter, due out next week, is expected to accelerate beyond 10% on year. Inflation is also making a comeback, hitting 2% on year in May after several years of stable prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Ron Harui and Kevin Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com; kevin.lim@dowjones.com \par    (Ron Harui, an Asia-Pacific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  correspondent in Singapore for Dow Jones Newswires, previously reported from Japan and the U.S.) \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 07-07-04 0552GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 20713 4258 5095  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040707e0770005j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
