\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Strong on yen, Fed move priced in.  \b0\par\par\uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2004\par 06:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Ovais Subhani\par     SINGAPORE, June 25 (Reuters) - Asian regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par were bid sharply higher on Friday following a rally in the yen\par to two-month highs against the dollar, prompting the South\par Korean won and Singapore dollar to hit multi-week peaks.\par     The won            rose to 1,150.5 per dollar, the highest\par since April 14, at the open of local trade. It then pulled back\par slightly on fears authorities might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the rise,\par dealers said.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar        rose more than half a percent\par to 1.7058 per U.S. dollar, last traded on June 9, and almost\par two cents above Tuesday's one-month low.\par     The Thai baht        hit 40.79, its highest since June 14,\par and the Taiwan dollar  saw 33.595 per dollar, its\par strongest in over a week.\par  \par     Even the Philippine peso        gained, strengthening past\par 56 per dollar for the first time since June 16.\par     "Several stops have been tripped and there is no dollar\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 demand from corporates or utilities that had been there in the  \par \par \uc2 past few sessions," said a dealer at a European bank in  \par \par \uc2 Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 The dealer said the dollar's losses prompted major foreign  \par \par \uc2 funds and investment banks to close out some long U.S. dollar  \par \par \uc2 positions against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Weak data, violence in Iraq, and bomb attacks in Turkey  \par \par \uc2 ahead of a visit by U.S. President George W. Bush saw markets  \par \par \uc2 sell the dollar on Thursday, and it remained under pressure on  \par \par \uc2 Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Apart from geo-political concerns, the dollar's fall showed  \par \par \uc2 that the markets might have fully factored in an expected rise  \par \par \uc2 in U.S. rates next week, analysts in Singapore said.  \par \par \uc2 "Price action is more a reflection of markets becoming more  \par \par \uc2 comfortable with the Fed outlook heading into a likely 25  \par \par \uc2 points move next week," Ashley Davies, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at  \par \par \uc2 UBS in Singapore, said in a client note.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The U.S. Federal Reserve holds a policy meeting on Tuesday\par and Wednesday and is widely expected to start raising rates.\par     However, many analysts expect concerns about funding the\par large U.S. current account deficit to weigh on the dollar as\par markets discount the shift in the Fed's monetary policy stance.\par     Claudio Piron, head of Standard Chartered's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy in\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore, said rising inflation in Asia would dampen the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 desire of the region's central banks to keep their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 weak and use stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tighten monetary  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 conditions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Asian policy makers could opt for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 offset rising inflationary pressures rather than allowing  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 interest rates to rise," Piron said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That argument has already come in focus in Singapore.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers said investors were taking a closer look at the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 trade-weighted value of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after Wednesday's consumer  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflation data showed annual inflation rising by two percent  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the steepest since December 2000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore tightened policy in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 April, citing the risk of a strong economic recovery stoking  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflation. Analysts believe the CPI numbers would reinforce  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 that stance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  targets the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within an  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 undisclosed trade-weighted band. In April it said it would  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 allow a gradual strengthening of the trade-weighted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Change on the day at 0300 GMT   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move\par  Japan yen     107.42       107.90         +0.45\par  Sing dlr      1.7057       1.7134         +0.45\par  Taiwan dlr    33.657       33.707         +0.15\par  Korean won   1151.80      1155.70         +0.34\par  Baht           40.79        40.90         +0.27\par  Peso           56.03        56.04         +0.01\par  Rupiah       9418.00      9425.00         +0.07\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2       Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     107.42       107.31         -0.10\par  Sing dlr      1.7057       1.6978         -0.46\par  Tai