\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Dollar at risk as inflation deters Asian  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .   \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Mair   \par \uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2004\par 11:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , June 24 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar may be heading for a big fall, with analysts saying that rising inflation will force Asian central banks to cut the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has been financing the massive U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 "The price to pay for the type of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we saw last year...is typically that you get inflationary pressure," said Jan Lambregts, Rabobank's head of Asia-Pacific research.   \par \par \uc2 "Last year that price did not have to be paid, but this time around, in an environment where inflationary pressure is on the rise, such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could really throw fuel on the fire."   \par \par \uc2 Last week's record U.S. current account deficit of $144.9 billion for the first quarter was a reminder to markets fixated on next week's U.S. Federal Reserve meeting that the United States still has a funding problem.   \par \par \uc2 In 2003 the United States ran a current account deficit of $530.6 billion - broadly, the imbalance between its exports and imports and the income it received from or paid to foreigners.   \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks were happy to finance that deficit by buying U.S. dollar assets, such as Treasury bonds, to hold down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep their exports competitive. In 2003, they increased their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by almost $520 billion.   \par \par \uc2 That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occured when deflation was a worry for much of Asia, which was also struggling with the impact of the war in Iraq and the SARS outbreak.   \par \par \uc2 Now deflation has gone in most Asian countries, where prices are not only rising but accelerating. On Wednesday, China said inflation was moving to near five percent, a rate seen as a possible trigger for a rate rise, while Singapore reported its highest inflation rate since December 2000.   \par \par \uc2 Allowing a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen is one way to slow an economy and hold down inflation. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down, on the other hand, tends to pump up the amount of money in the economy, stoking inflation.   \par \par \uc2 LESS AGGRESSIVE   \par \par \uc2 "With inflationary pressures starting to pick up, it's a bit more of a two-way street as to what you should let your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  do," Standard Chartered Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Claudio Piron said.   \par \par \uc2 "I think that is perhaps a rationale why we may see Asian central banks being less aggressive in terms of supporting the dollar in coming quarters, especially if oil prices remain high."   \par \par \uc2 Prospective U.S. rate rises have pushed the current account deficit into the background, but it should reappear as the market becomes more comfortable with rising rates.   \par \par \uc2 "We would argue that the latest trade, current account and balance of payments data dramatically emphasise the negative case for the dollar," Jim O'Neill, head of global research at Goldman Sachs, said in a report.   \par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs estimates that to cut the current account deficit to three percent of GDP from about five percent now, the dollar would need to fall by about 20 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Looking at the dollar from another angle, Goldman Sachs reckoned that a 31 percent fall would be needed to make U.S. assets attractive enough for foreigners to support it.   \par \par \uc2 In recent years Asia has blocked such a correction for fear of losing export competitiveness. So in the first quarter, for example, the United States could turn to Asia to fund its current account needs of $1.6 billion a day.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. ASSETS   \par \par \uc2 In these three months Japan spent a record 14.83 trillion yen - about $138 billion based on the average dollar/yen rate for the quarter - trying to prop up the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 All up, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at Asia's central banks rose by $247 billion in the first quarter. Most of that is thought to have been invested in U.S. assets.   \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks have already scaled down their dollar buying, but not because of inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Instead, the reason appears to have been that the dollar rose in April and May because investors bet on an early rise in U.S. interest rates. So \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not needed to hold Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down.   \par \par \uc2 Since March, Japan has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Excluding China, which has not released figures yet, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by only $2 billion in the two months to May 31.   \par \par \uc2 Economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen again - and it certainly will not disappear entirely - but inflation should prevent Asia's central banks from being such willing financiers of the U.S. current account.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040624e06o0010v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
