\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late On Increased Terrorism Concerns  \b0\par\par\uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2004\par 10:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger late Monday in Asia on increased concerns about terrorism in the wake of a suspected al-Qaida attack on foreigners working in Saudi Arabia's oil industry over the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were lower, following the U.S. Treasurys' weaker close late Friday, in quiet trade as U.S. markets were shut for a holiday Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0800 GMT, the U.S. unit was quoted at S$1.6975, down from S$1.6990 late Friday in Asia. It traded a S$1.6940-S$1.7000 range intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the U.S. dollar swiftly slipped from its opening level of S$1.7000 to its morning low of S$1.6975 following news of the weekend rampage by gunmen that killed 22 people, including several foreigners, in the Saudi city of Khobar.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants were concerned that the weekend rampage could fuel tensions in the Middle East and might be a prelude to a possible terrorist-related attack in the U.S. on its Memorial Day holiday today.  \par \par \uc2 Only fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb excessive volatility of the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair prevented the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from extending its slide against its Singaporean counterpart in the morning, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 "Players were cautious about pushing (the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar) down further due to (ongoing) rumors that the authorities had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday" to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair, said a local dealer.  \par \par \uc2 These rumors were sparked Friday when the U.S. dollar recovered somewhat after falling to a three-week low of S$1.6960 early in that day's session.  \par \par \uc2 But ongoing worries over terrorism, rising instability in the Middle East and growing notions that the Federal Reserve's expected interest-rate hikes are already factored into the markets kept the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar under downward pressure, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Later in the Asian session, the U.S. dollar slipped further from around S$1.6975 to an intraday low of S$1.6940, its lowest since May 7, as stop-loss sales orders in the mid-S$1.6900s were triggered by the U.S. unit's persistent weakness against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 According to technical analysis, the U.S. dollar remains short-term bearish against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week after it broke below several important support levels last week, including its uptrend line from mid-April and Fibonacci support at S$1.7041 (the 38.2% retracement of the rise from mid-April).  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. unit held quite strong support at S$1.6958-S$1.6959 (the 50- and 100-day moving averages and the 50% retracement) last Friday and for most of today, its break below this area late in today's session means it could target the 61.8% retracement level of S$1.6875 in coming days. [ 31-05-04 0829GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds drifted lower at the start of the local session, following a weaker close in U.S. Treasurys late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 But trade in the local market wound down soon after the early slide, with market participants holding positions ahead of a holiday in the U.S. Monday.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the session, the yield on the two-year government bond was 1.11%, up from late Friday's close of 1.09%. The yield on the 10-year paper rose to 3.30% versus 3.27%, while the 15-year bond yield rose one basis point to end at 3.84%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were also slightly higher, in line with local bond yields. The two-year swap offer was at 1.65% from 1.61% late Friday. The five-year swap was at 2.70% from 2.65% previously, while the 10-year swap was at 3.70% from 3.66% late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps was unchanged at 205 basis points from late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The interbank overnight rate slipped to 0.4375% from 0.5000%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change.  \par \par \uc2 Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 31-05-04 0845GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040531e05v00095\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
