\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Down Late On Signs Of U.S. Inflation  \b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2004\par 09:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was weaker late Friday, having hit a fresh six-month low overnight and again during Asian trading as signs of inflation in the U.S. sparked heavy buying of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were also lower on U.S. inflation fears as the market awaited the U.S. April consumer price index due later in the global day.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0715 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7273, up from S$1.7240 late Thursday in Asia. It traded between S$1.7245 and S$1.7305 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit touched a six-month high of S$1.7294 late Thursday in New York, its highest level since Nov. 13. It rose further to S$1.7305 Friday afternoon in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Although it fell slightly from its opening level of around S$1.7269 on profit-taking early in the session, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon began to climb toward the psychologically key S$1.7300 level on buying by domestic and non-Singaporean players, who were betting on a further U.S. dollar rise in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 They based their bets partly on a stronger-than-expected U.S. producer price index for April, which increased expectations that the Federal Reserve could hike interest rates soon.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Labor Department reported Thursday that the PPI rose 0.7% in April, well above the consensus forecast for a 0.3% increase. The PPI excluding food and energy rose 0.2%, in line with expectations.  \par \par \uc2 Although energy prices fueled the PPI rise, some economists fear these energy price increases could spill over into the consumer sector. Those fears are likely to take centerstage when the U.S. April CPI is released at 1230 GMT. Economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC project a 0.3% rise in April CPI and a 0.2% increase when food and energy prices are taken out.  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. dollar ran into strong psychological and technical resistance in the S$1.7293-SS1.7300 zone, the U.S. unit later climbed to S$1.7305 on persistent buying by U.S. investment houses.  \par \par \uc2 Growing concerns over inflationary pressures in the U.S., the negative impact on Asia from high oil prices and diverging interest-rate paths between the U.S. and Asian nations in favor of the U.S. spurred the U.S. banks' purchases of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Late in the Asian session, however, the U.S. dollar fell back to around S$1.7270 on selling by local banks. One dealer cited vague speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the rapid fall of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although others said the banks' sales may have been just profit-taking ahead of the U.S. CPI data. [ 14-05-04 0738GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Late Friday, the yield on the two-year government bond was 1.15%, up from Thursday's 1.12%. The 10-year yield was also higher at 3.51% against 3.44% previously, while the 15-year yield was 3.96% versus 3.91%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were also up. The two-year swap offer rose to 1.77% from 1.71% late Thursday, while the five-year swap offer was at 2.85% from 2.79%. The 10-year swap offer was at 3.85% versus 3.79% previously.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps was flat from Thursday at 208 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 The interbank overnight rate rose to 0.7500% from 0.6875%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Ron Harui and Kevin Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Dawn Lye [ 14-05-04 0805GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040514e05e000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
