\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Steady Late Vs US Dlr After Choppy Trade  \b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2004\par 10:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was relatively steady against its U.S. counterpart late Friday after choppy trading ahead of this weekend's meeting of finance ministers and central bankers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations in Washington.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds were higher, following U.S. Treasurys' gains in New York and Tokyo as investors tempered their expectations for any move by the U.S. Federal Reserve to raise interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 At 0740 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6950, nearly flat from S$1.6955 late Thursday. It traded between S$1.6953 and S$1.7010 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the U.S. dollar hit a five-week high of S$1.7010 as domestic and non-Singaporean players bought the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bet on an increase in U.S. interest rates in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 Despite a smaller-than-anticipated fall in U.S. weekly jobless claims released late Thursday, expectations remained high that the Federal Reserve would raise interest rates this summer. The latest weekly jobless claims fell by 9,000 but economists were expecting a fall of 20,000.  \par \par \uc2 But some local banks immediately sold into the U.S. dollar's surge above the psychologically important S$1.7000 level in order to take profits prior to this weekend's G7 meeting, dragging the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back down to the mid-S$1.6900s.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the G7 meeting's wording on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stay basically unchanged from the previous statement in Boca Raton, given that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trading at levels broadly acceptable to the U.S., Japan and Europe in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 finance officials at the Boca Raton meeting in February said they generally supported flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rates but expressed concern over "disorderly" fluctuations.  \par \par \uc2 The early afternoon release of Singapore's consumer price index for March, which rose 1.3% from a year ago, had little impact on the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair as the data were largely in line with market expectations for an on-year rise of 1.4%, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 With the city-state's inflation staying relatively steady for the first three months of the year, there are few signs so far that price pressures are building here, which could give the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  less reason to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar weakness, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 In the short term, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's outlook is still bullish, as technical analysis shows that its rise above S$1.6894 Wednesday has triggered a reverse head and shoulders pattern on the daily chart, targeting S$1.7160. [ 23-04-04 0808GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds ended higher Friday, tracking the movements of U.S. Treasurys in New York and Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore dealers said local bond prices managed to recover from sharp falls earlier this week when U.S. Treasurys tumbled following comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan which raised the possibility of a U.S. interest rate hike sometime this year.  \par \par \uc2 Local bonds, however, responded little to the Singapore March CPI data, mainly because the 1.3% on-year rise was within expectations, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of local session, the yield on the two-year government bond was at 1.03%, down from the previous close of 1.05%. The 10-year yield was at 3.27% against 3.33% previously, while the 15-year yield was nine basis points lower at 3.71%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were, however, mixed with the two-year swap offer unchanged at 1.57%, the five-year swap offer at 2.69% from 2.71% late Thursday, while the 10-year swap offer was at 3.69% from 3.76%.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 212 basis points from late Thursday's 219 basis points.  \par \par \uc2 The interbank overnight rate rose to 0.5000% versus 0.3750% late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Ron Harui and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; djnews.singapore.bureau@dowjones.com [ 23-04-04 0845GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040423e04n000cp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
