\par\par \b \uc2 MAS Econ Review: Singapore Dlr Up 3% Vs US Dlr In Oct-Mar  \b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2004\par 06:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was higher by around 3% against the U.S. dollar at the end of March from where it was at the start of the fourth quarter in 2003, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said in its semiannual macroeconomic review Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the Singapore dollar nominal effective exchange rate, or NEER, has been under general upward pressure since late last year, reflecting the firmer economic recovery in Singapore and the region and the U.S. dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The call of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates also led to an influx of short-term capital, the MAS said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0220 GMT Tuesday, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.6800 compared with S$1.6988 at the start of the year. It hit a four-year low of S$1.6608 on April 13.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS reiterated that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and to curb excessive volatility, thereby keeping the trade-weighed index fairly stable within the policy band.  \par \par \uc2 The more recent appreciation of the Singapore dollar NEER toward the upper half of the policy band was supported by positive economic data, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added.  \par \par \uc2 While the MAS didn't disclose any forecasts for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its macroeconomic review, analysts expect further Singapore dollar appreciation in the coming months after the MAS unexpectedly tightened monetary policy April 12.  \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase Bank cut its year-end forecast for the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair to S$1.6200 from S$1.6500 projected previously, while IDEAglobal economist Nizam Idris forecast the U.S. dollar to drift down to S$1.6400 by then.  \par \par \uc2 In its biannual monetary policy statement issued last week, the MAS said it would shift from a zero percent appreciation path to a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar NEER, starting from the midpoint of the current policy band, without changing the width of the band.  \par \par \uc2 Since October last year, the general strengthening in global economic activity, coupled with the uncertainty over the outlook for the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has given \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading activity in Singapore a significant boost, the MAS said.  \par \par \uc2 Compared with the year-ago period, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading turnover rose 22% in the October 2003-February 2004 period, with the bulk of the increase attributed to transactions of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the euro, the Australian dollar and East Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 Of the instruments traded, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps accounted for around 49% while spot transactions represented about 39% of total turnover, with the rest attributed to forward contracts.  \par \par \uc2 -By Izham Ahmad and Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; izham.ahmad@dowjones.com, ron.harui@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ryan Woo [ 20-04-04 0400GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040420e04k0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
