\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore To Tighten Monetary Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2004\par 02:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--In a surprise move, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Monday said it will tighten monetary policy by shifting from neutral to allow a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Against a more favourable growth outlook for the Singapore economy, and the risk of rising inflationary pressures, MAS is shifting from a 0% appreciation path to a policy of modest and gradual appreciation of the S$NEER, starting from the mid-point of the current policy band," MAS said.  \par \par \uc2 The width of the band will remain unchanged, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added. [ 12-04-04 0007GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Most economists had expected Singapore to continue with a neutral monetary policy until the next policy statement in October, although they said there was a chance MAS may signal tolerance of a relatively firm Singapore dollar against its nominal effective exchange rate, or NEER.  \par \par \uc2 Because of the open nature of the island's trade-driven economy, MAS uses the Singapore dollar's exchange rate against an undisclosed basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, to support economic growth while maintaining domestic price stability.  \par \par \uc2 In its statement Monday, MAS also revised its forecast for consumer-price inflation in 2004 to 1.5%-2.0%, from 0.5%-1.5% previously.  \par \par \uc2 "Consumer price inflation has trended up in recent months, averaging 1.4% year-on-year in January-February 2004, compared to 0.5% and 0.7% in the last two quarters of 2003, respectively, " MAS said.  \par \par \uc2 It said the increase largely reflected supply-side factors, including the passthrough effects of global commodity-related price increases and the goods-and-services tax hike in January 2004.  \par \par \uc2 It added: "Domestic price pressures remain subdued but could intensify if the growth momentum remains strong over the next few quarters, causing the labor market to tighten."  \par \par \uc2 MAS also said the Singapore dollar "NEER has generally been under upward pressure since late last year, reflecting the firmer economic recovery in Singapore and the region, and the weakness of the U.S. dollar. The G7's call for more flexible exchange rates also led to an influx of short-term capital."  \par \par \uc2 MAS had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to moderate the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and to damp excessive volatility," it said.  \par \par \uc2 "The Singapore dollar NEER remained fairly stable until January, but more recently has appreciated towards the upper end of the policy band, supported by the release of encouraging economic data including on exports and industrial production," it added.  \par \par \uc2 On the economy, MAS said the Ministry of Trade and Industry has assessed that the gross domestic product growth range for 2004 is likely to exceed the current forecast of 3.5-5.5%.  \par \par \uc2 "While downside risks to the outlook remain, on balance, the upturn in the global economy and IT markets is likely to be sustained," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added.  \par \par \uc2 -By Kevin Lim, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415 4156; kevin.lim@dowjones.com [ 12-04-04 0044GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040412e04c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
