\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Apr 6  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 April 2004\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's markets moved higher Tuesday as investors heaved a sigh of relief that parliamentary elections passed without violence. But the longer-term outlook for the stock market and the rupiah remains clouded by likely months of political infighting ahead, analysts said. ("=INDONESIA VOTE: Mkts Rise But Split Vote Causes Jitters," ran at 0659 GMT; UPDATE will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Strong U.S. economic data are making an interest rate hike there more likely even as the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be leaning toward a policy easing to revive economic growth. That means the euro could weaken further, say a growing number of analysts. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia:Shifting Rate, Econ Growth Gaps To Hurt Euro/Dlr," ran at 0403 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: A ruling by the Chinese government Tuesday appeared to set back hopes for democratic reform in Hong Kong, which could dent the confidence of some investors in the territory. But other investors and businessmen saw the ruling as positive, because it means no quick change to the business-oriented status quo that has underpinned Hong Kong's economic success of the past few decades ("China Ruling Protects Hong Kong Status Quo," will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total of Y1.4185 trillion of its most recent retail government bond issue, making April's the largest such quarterly issue ever, a ministry official said Tuesday. ("MOF April Retail JGB Issuance Largest Ever At Y1.419 Tln," ran at 0709 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =BOJ WATCH: The Bank of Japan's policy board will likely leave ultra-easy monetary settings steady when it meets Thursday and Friday, judging that rises in the yen and long-term bond rates since the last meeting aren't large enough to derail Japan's economic recovery. ("=BOJ Watch: To Keep Policy Steady Despite Yen, Rate Rises," ran at 0744 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The emergence of a widening slowdown in the domestic economy will ensure the Reserve Bank of Australia leaves official interest rates unchanged for a fourth straight month at its policy meeting Tuesday. If interest rates are changed, the decision will be announced at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy announcement window at 2330 GMT on Wednesday. ("=PREVIEW: Australian Rates On Hold As Slowdown Emerges," ran at 0449 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's New South Wales state government Tuesday exempted most first home buyers from stamp duty, complicating matters for the Reserve Bank as it seeks to damp consumer demand for credit. ("=UPDATE: Australia's NSW State Taxes Could Stoke Housing," ran at 0738 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's retail sales surged in February thanks to a continuing revival in consumer demand and growth in inbound tourism, the government said Tuesday. Retail sales leaped 13.2% by value and 11.4% by volume during the month, following respective rises of 4.2% and 3.0% in January. ("DATA SNAP: Hong Kong's Retail Sales Surge In February," ran at 0830 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Inflation in the Philippines accelerated in March, heightening expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may tighten monetary policy, though not immediately. Separate data released Tuesday showed exports rose 7.5% on year in February to $2.999 billion, more than the 4.1% growth posted in January, fueled by higher shipments of electronics. ("=UPDATE: Philippine Mar CPI Boosts Rate Hike Expectations," ran at 0813 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The Philippine government's foreign commercial borrowing needs for 2005 are expected to be similar to this year's $1.8 billion-$1.85 billion range, with the possibility of slightly more debt in order to build up the country's foreign reserves, said Finance Undersecretary Eric Recto. ("=INTERVIEW:Philippine 05 Foreign Debt To Be Similar To 04," ran at 0354 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Rampant misuse of investor funds by Chinese brokerage firms is prompting regulators to consider radical new protections, including a proposal reported Tuesday that would bar firms from holding client funds at all. ("China May End Brokers' Control Of Client Funds - Report," ran at 0349 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: South Korea's sickly domestic sector may be starting to recover, inflationary pressures building, and the global economic outlook strengthening - but few economists expect the Bank of Korea to tighten policy any time soon. Indeed, economists are increasingly tilting toward an interest rate hike only after 2004. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Korea Rate Hike More Likely After 04," ran at 0556 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Producer prices in South Korea rose for the ninth consecutive month in March due to higher prices of imported oil and other raw materials such as fresh food, the Bank of Korea said Tuesday. ("S Korea Mar Producer Prices +0.9% On Mo; Up 9 Mos In Row," ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Seven out of 10 South Korean small-size manufacturers face losses in selling products overseas due to sharp won gains against the U.S. dollar so far this year, according to a survey by Korea International Trade Association Tuesday. ("S Korea Small Exporters Face Losses From Firm Won -Survey," ran at 0416 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Hoping to pull the rug out from under his wily opponent, Australian Labor Party leader Mark Latham's decision to wade into the political hotspot of national security appears to have backfired instead. ("=UPDATE: Australian Labor's Security Pitch Falls Flat," ran at 0336 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar rose against the yen in Asia Monday, boosted by crosstrading pressure as the euro trimmed losses against the yen late in the day. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Up Vs Yen On Crosstrade Boost From Eur/Yen," ran at 0604 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's Qantas Airways Tuesday unveiled bold plans for a budget Asian airline to commence \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of the year. The no-frills carrier, which will be majority owned by Qantas, will face stiff competition from heavyweight Singapore Airlines and its budget carrier Tiger Airways, also slated for take off by year end, and will compete as well with Malaysia's budget carrier AirAsia and Valueair, a budget airline being set up by former SIA executives. Singapore government investment arm Temasek Holdings will hold a 19% stake in the yet-to-be-named budget airline. ("Qantas, Singapore's Temasek Form Budget Airline For Asia," ran at 0402 GMT; UPDATE will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The news lately from Singapore Airlines has been anything but good. Industrial relations at the world's second-largest airline by market capitalization are so poor that \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Senior Minister, Lee Kuan Yew, has twice \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  personally to iron out differences. The threat from low-cost airlines is also growing, with Australian rival Qantas Tuesday announcing plans for a new low-cost carrier to be partially funded by SIA's largest shareholder, Temasek Holdings. While Singapore Airlines will report a healthy profit in the fiscal year ended March 31, and also in FY2004-2005, analysts say the airline faces growing pressure to restructure. ("=Pressure Mounts On Singapore Air As Qantas Enters Turf," ran at 0830 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 COLOMBO: Sri Lanka's stock market took a severe beating Tuesday, with the key index ending down 9.7% at 1186.03 amid worries that President Chandrika Kumaratunga's win at the elections will be bad for the economy and efforts to end a 20-year civil war. ("=Sri Lanka Shares Tumble On Election Outcome," first take ran at 0523 GMT; UPDATE will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Haier-CCT Holdings said it will buy from China's Haier Group a washing machine business and its stake in a mobile handset joint venture for a total consideration of about HK$1.50 billion in cash, shares and bonds. The deal will result in Haier Group taking over the Hong Kong-listed company. ("China Haier Group Takes Over Haier-CCT In HK$1.5B Deal," first take ran at 0006 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The Taiwan government and Chinatrust Group each secured seven seats on China Development Financial's board during a vote at a shareholders' meeting Monday, while incumbent chairwoman Diana Chen won only four seats and other major shareholders secured the remaining three seats. That ended months of battle for control of the board of the financial group that owns Taiwan's largest investment bank. But China Development Financial's newly elected board members have yet to decide on the new chairman and lineup of the management team. ("=UPDATE:Taiwan Govt Wants Chen Mu-tzai As China Devt Head," ran at 0254 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Japan's market for real-estate investment trusts, or REITs, has been on a tear, as investors pump in money in the hope that the country's long struggle with deflation could be ending. But increasingly, analysts are saying the market has climbed too high. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Has Surging Japan REIT Market Peaked?" ran at 0126 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial says it will use more than one third of the Y120 billion in IT investment it has earmarked for the next three years for supply-chain-management systems, particularly in Asia where its manufacturing bases are centered. ("Matsushita To Focus On Asia Systems In 3-Yr Y120B IT Plan," ran at 0843 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Tokio Marine & Fire Insurance has set up a new sales base in North America for the first time in 24 years, in a move to gain new customers among the automobile and autoparts makers in the area such as Toyota Motor. ("Tokio Marine Opens 1st Branch In N America In 24 Years," ran at 0248 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Asahi Glass said Tuesday it will invest up to $100 million to build an automobile-use glass plant in China, which will be the glassmaker's second flat glass plant in that country. ("Asahi Glass To Build China Auto Glass Plant For $100 Mln," ran at 0720 GMT) [ 06-04-04 0904GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Seven-Eleven Japan plans to open a chain store in Beijing on April 15, the company's joint venture said Tuesday, marking the first of five new outlets to be opened in China's capital before the end of May. ("Seven-Eleven Japan's China JV To Open 1st Beijing Store," ran at 0809 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: United Overseas Bank, Singapore's second-largest bank by assets, said Tuesday it may buy a 23% stake in PT Bank Buana Indonesia from the Indonesian bank's major shareholder. ("Singapore's UOB Says May Buy 23% Of Indonesia's Bk Buana," ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Press Holdings, the island's dominant media group, will likely report fiscal first-half net profit fell to S$153 million from S$166.5 million a year earlier, mainly due to the sluggish recovery in advertising spending. (results expected around 0900 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Craig Barrett, chief executive of Intel Corp., the world's biggest semiconductor maker, Tuesday said it's too early to predict the outcome of an ongoing dispute between wireless technology providers and the Chinese authorities. ("Intel CEO: Too Early To Predict China Chip Dispute Impact," ran at 0654 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Tokyo Electron raised its earnings forecasts for the year ended March 31, citing stronger-than-expected sales of semiconductor production equipment and improving profitability. ("Tokyo Electron Ups Last FY Group Net Pft Outlook To Y8B," first take ran at 0647 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The locals haven't had much to celebrate during Japan's long economic slump. But increasing signs of solidness in the recent economic rebound appear to have led consumers to raise not only their hopes, but also their beer glasses. ("=Japan Beer Sales To Buck Up As Drinkers Toast Economy," ran at 0415 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: After suffering for years amid weak sales, Japanese retailers are finally perking up. Rising consumer spending, easing deflation and aggressive cost cuts likely lifted sales and profits for Ito-Yokado Co. and Daimaru Inc. in the fiscal year ended February. ("=JAPAN SECTOR OUTLOOK: Retailers Pfts Finally Perking Up," ran at 0606 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Kentucky Fried Chicken Japan Ltd. has cut its earnings estimate for the year ending Nov. 30 due to a drop in sales following the outbreak of bird flu in Japan in January. ("Kentucky Fried Chicken Japan Cuts Pft Outlook On Bird Flu," ran at 0844 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Property-to-shipping company Shun Tak Holdings said net profit fell 13.4% last year, pulled down by higher property-related costs and a loss on investments. Net profit for the 12 months ended Dec. 31, 2003, was HK$327.2 million, compared with a restated net profit of HK$378 million a year earlier. ("HK Shun Tak FY Net HK$327.2 Mln Vs HK$378.0 Mln," first take ran at 0038 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Eastern Airlines said it swung to a deep loss last year largely as a result of the impact on revenue from the SARS outbreak last spring. The Shanghai-based airline, one of China's top three carriers, reported a net loss of CNY949.8 million compared with a net profit of CNY86.4 million a year earlier - its worst result since listing in 1997. ("China Eastern FY Loss CNY949.8 Mln Vs Profit CNY86.4 Mln," first take ran at 0021 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Automobile and airplane maker AviChina Industry & Technology reported double-digit growth in its net profit last year as revenue from principle \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  - particularly airplanes - rose. Net profit rose 14% to CNY452.1 million from CNY396.7 million a year earlier as revenue jumped to CNY15.07 billion from CNY11.31 billion. ("AviChina Full-Year Net Pft CNY452.1 Mln Vs CNY396.7 Mln," ran at 0023 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Private investment company Rural Portfolio Investments said Tuesday it plans to win control of New Zealand's largest rural servicing company Wrightson Ltd. with a partial takeover bid. Rural Portfolio, headed by the former chief executive of Fonterra Cooperative Group, Craig Norgate, already owns around 13% of Wrightson and said in a statement to the New Zealand Exchange that it is planning to offer NZ$1.50 per share to up its stake to 50.01%. ("UPDATE: NZ Rural Portfolio To Make Bid For Wrightson," ran at 0043 GMT )  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Despite watching nickel prices plunge 20% over the past three months, major producer WMC Resources said Tuesday that the metal needs to retreat further to maintain a "sustainable" industry. ("=UPDATE: Australia's WMC Says Nickel Needs Further Fall," ran at 0805 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Six Japanese companies, with government financial backing, have started planning the possible reconstruction of war-torn liquefied petroleum gas production plants in southern Iraq. This plant reconstruction could help pave the way for energy-poor Japan to tap into other rehabilitation projects in Iraq involving crude oil production and refinery \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=Japan Eyes Reconstruction Of War-Torn Gas Plants In Iraq," ran at 0809 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Spurred on by an upbeat outlook for copper prices, BHP Billiton has approved an US$870 million project to process lower grade ore, boosting output at Chile's giant Escondida copper mine. (UPDATE will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: National elections are a time when every political party in India wants to champion the cause of the country's millions of farmers who also form the bulk of the electorate. It has been no different this time as India prepares for nationwide polls starting April 20. ("=Indian Parties Promise Ag Reform If Voted To Power" ran at 0249 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ISLAMABAD: Weak international cotton prices and a hefty anti-dumping duty imposed on Pakistani bedlinen by the European Union will likely slow the country's key textile exports in the months ahead, analysts said Tuesday. ("=Weak Cotton Prices,Duty To Hurt Pakistan Textile Exports," ran at 0844 GMT) [ 06-04-04 0904GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040406e046000ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
