\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Sharply Higher Vs US Dlr Late As Yen Surges  \b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 11:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar continued to gain sharply Wednesday, touching a two-month high of S$1.6750 against its U.S. counterpart as the greenback went into a free fall against the Japanese yen.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds were barely changed in thin trading as the market awaited a U.S. employment report due at the end of this week. Traders said the outlook for bonds remains bearish.  \par \par \uc2 At 0810 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at S$1.6756, down from S$1.6838 late Tuesday in Asia. It changed hands between S$1.6750 and S$1.6830 during the day.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said that the main sellers of U.S. dollars against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were Japanese banks, and that there was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  during Asian trading.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was initially supported at S$1.6780 in the morning, but dropped below this level in the afternoon after the greenback failed to stay above its Y104.50 support against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 According to Tokyo traders, the dollar selling Wednesday was triggered by U.S.-based hedge funds on growing market speculation that Tokyo has abandoned its policy of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yen from appreciating sharply.  \par \par \uc2 Despite verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials, including Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, who said the government would take steps against excessive movements if necessary, the dollar's fall was exacerbated by stop-loss sell orders and new sell orders coming out of Europe.  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the dollar was trading at Y103.82, down sharply from Y105.64 late Tuesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the Singapore dollar's performance going forward will depend on movements in the U.S. dollar-yen pair.  \par \par \uc2 Although the MAS is expected to stick to its neutral monetary policy stance next month, some players believe the authority will allow the local unit to trade in a wider band against the trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  given higher commodity prices that will likely lead to inflationary pressures. [ 31-03-04 0938GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Late Wednesday, the yield on the two-year Singapore government securities was 0.98%, marginally higher compared with Tuesday's 0.97%. At the longer end of the curve, the yield on 10-year bonds was 3.08%, against 3.06% previously, while the 15-year yield was unchanged at 3.46%.  \par \par \uc2 Interest rate swaps were flat. The two- and five-year swap offers were unchanged at 1.35% and 2.41%, respectively, while the 10-year swap offer was at 3.43% against 3.44% late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The spread between two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 208 basis points from 209 basis points late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight rate rose to 0.6875% from 0.5625%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices quoted are pre-settlement bids obtained from dealers and are subject to change. Historical bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Kevin Lim and Izham Ahmad, Dow Jones Newswires;  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 65-6415-4150; kevin.lim@dowjones.com [ 31-03-04 0941GMT ]  